<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panakas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as panaca, panaqa, panaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The panakas were the royal kin groups of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, each formed by the descendants of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> or his wife: a noble form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayllu">ayllu</a>, the Andean kinship group descended from a common ancestor. The crown prince did not belong to his father's panaka; on becoming emperor he left his original panaka and formed his own. The panakas made up the court and aristocracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, held estates and palaces across the realm, tended shrines along the ceque lines and kept the mummy (mallki) of their founder, in whose name they took part in politics. Eleven are listed, five of Hurin (lower) and six of Hanan (upper) Cusco, and scholars still dispute what the word first meant.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panakas.jpg" alt="Panakas" width="330" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cuzco School · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuzco_school_-_Genealogy_of_the_Incas_-_18th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Each dead ruler’s mummy was kept and served by his descendants, organised in a royal kin group or <b>panaqa</b> that held his estates; these groups became powerful factions in politics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Inca nobles were the descendants of the rulers of Cusco, grouped in royal lineages (<b>panaqa</b>).</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li></ul></section>
